	doi = {10.1109/PCS65673.2025.11417508},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035833698?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Asymmetric video encoding, in which different regions of a frame are encoded at varying quality levels, offers a bitrate-efficient means of preserving perceptual quality. However, the Video Multi-method Assessment Fusion (VMAF) metric - one of the leading objective video quality metrics - shows limitations in accurately evaluating quality under asymmetric encoding conditions. In this paper, we propose novel methods to improve VMAF's correlation with subjective quality scores for asymmetrically encoded videos. Our approach incorporates nonlinear transformations and models interactions between VMAF scores across different regions of a video frame to enhance predictive accuracy. To validate our methods, we constructed a new subjective video quality dataset and conducted comprehensive subjective assessments, confirming that VMAF underperforms in asymmetric scenarios. Using this dataset, we developed advanced prediction models that consistently outperform traditional metrics such as PSNR and standard VMAF in terms of correlation with subjective scores and their ability to rank encoding quality. These results highlight the effectiveness of our approach and its potential as a reliable solution for adaptive video encoding. © 2025 IEEE.},
