	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85183050854?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This work-in-progress paper presents a proposal for eliciting and specifying software requirements using the technique of mind maps. The objective of the study was to investigate whether the application of mind maps improves the understanding and documentation of user and system requirements. To achieve this objective, a case study was conducted in an industry with the participation of students from a Computer Science course, where students had the opportunity to observe the requirements gathering process for a real software. The students were organized into groups, with two experienced requirements engineering professors acting as mentors. Evaluation of the study included a questionnaire to assess the satisfaction level of the key and indirect users involved in the research and the average satisfaction score was very positive, indicating a high degree of satisfaction with the employed mind mapping technique. Additionally, a comprehensive evaluation was conducted to gather qualitative and quantitative data on the students' learning experience, including their opinions on the case study. The results revealed a high level of overall satisfaction among the students, with a majority expressing appreciation for the case study approach and its positive impact on their skills development in requirements engineering. Our findings highlight the benefits of incorporating mind maps in software engineering education, particularly in enhancing communication, comprehension, and collaboration among stakeholders. The study's case study approach, combined with industry engagement, provided students with practical experience and valuable insights into real-world requirements engineering challenges. © 2023 IEEE.},
