	abstract = {Requirements elicitation is a central but often timeconsuming activity in Requirements Engineering (RE), since elicited requirement information frequently needs to be analyzed, clarified, and refined over multiple iterative elicitation and validation cycles before reaching a sufficiently high level of quality. Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) enable new forms of conversational requirements elicitation through automated stakeholder interviews. This dissertation aims to investigate whether integrating LLM-based requirement quality assurance techniques into an LLM-based interviewer can improve both requirement quality and conversational interview guidance. The proposed approach continuously analyzes elicited requirement information during ongoing interviews in order to identify Requirement Quality Issues that influence the generation of follow-up questions and guide the conversational elicitation process. Within the scope of this thesis, research contributions are planned that investigate the extraction and representation of conversationally elicited requirement knowledge, the application of LLM-based requirement quality assurance techniques such as Perspective-Based Reading and checklist- or review-based approaches, and their integration into an autonomous LLMbased interviewer approach.  © 2026 IEEE.},
