	journal = {Proceedings of 2026 1st International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Automation for Sustainable Solutions, ICASS 2026},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICASS69550.2026.11547791},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042928521?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Data warehouses (DWs) are essential for strategic decision making in the organisation, requiring quality considerations from the initial phases of their development. The DW requirements data model is particularly important, as it has direct implications on later design decisions and influences the overall quality of DW systems. Inadequacies at early stages frequently spread to subsequent stages, increasing complexity, leading to higher costs, and decreasing the analytical efficacy of a system. Researchers have given due consideration to assessing quality through conceptual, logical and physical models of DWs; however, evaluation of requirements data models has received limited attention. Notably, the quality metrics/methodological frameworks for measuring and validating requirements data models remain inadequately explored. This study provides a detailed review of existing metrics proposed to measure quality of DW system at requirements level. The reviewed metrics are analysed with respect to the quality attributes they address and the validation (theoretical/empirical) approaches employed. It highlights existing gaps and provides a foundation for future directions toward more rigorous and effective evaluation of DW requirements models.  © 2026 IEEE.},
