	abstract = {Software Requirement Specifications (SRS) are typically written in natural language and may contain various requirement bad smells, including imprecise pronouns, missing information indicators, comparative expressions, and non-atomic requirements. The presence of these issues reduces the clarity of SRS documents, limiting their effectiveness for further automation tasks such as automated modelling, testing etc. The existing research primarily focused on a limited number of SRS documents and the identification of a restricted range of bad smells within requirement documents. This paper studies the prevalence of requirement bad smells in industry-level SRS documents and introduces an automated approach for their detection and mitigation. The proposed method employs rule-based linguistic pattern recognition to detect ten different categories of requirement bad smells and uses generative models, i.e., Gemini API to find solutions to fix incorrect requirement statements. The experiment has been conducted on publicly available datasets, i.e., the PURE dataset, which comprises seventy-nine SRS documents with 34,268 sentences. The study identified 25,266 instances of requirement bad smell, comprising 2,147 passive voice constructions, 1,589 conditional modal expressions, 3,638 missing information indicators, 11,310 ambiguous pronoun occurrences, 749 comparative terms, 3,903 non-atomic requirements, 1,115 undefined abbreviations, and 815 subjective expressions. The results show that bad smells are common in real-world specifications. Automatic detection and mitigation of bad smells could make the requirements document clearer and easier to comprehend. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
