	abstract = {Effective requirements development during the concept design phase is critical to the success of engineering projects. Despite its importance, it has received scant attention within conventional project management theory and practice. Nonetheless, since the emergence of artificial intelligence and its ability to solve problems, attention is now shifting towards problem finding, problem framing and consequently requirements engineering. This paper presents a case study based on real-world experiences in Cummins Inc. It defines the challenges in the requirements development process and presents a structured approach to capturing and managing requirements in the early stages of the project life cycle. The study demonstrates how a user-centred systematic approach enhances stakeholder alignment, improves decision quality, and mitigates project risks. Findings suggest that treating requirements development as a strategic project management process, rather than a purely technical task, leads to more robust project outcomes. By presenting a structured framework and practical insights, this research contributes to the body of knowledge on front-end project planning and advances the integration of engineering design and engineering project management disciplines. © 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
