	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0031106264?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To effectively implement the design controls required by Quality System regulation, medical device manufacturers need to structure and streamline their design and development process and integrate specific design controls at appropriate stages of the process. Procedures should be written and understood by all levels of the organization to ensure the adequate control of the design and development activities. These procedures should ensure that the following elements are part of the product development process: respect to the enforcement of design control regulations, the FDA must ensure that investigators are adequately trained to provide a consistent and reasonable assessment of a manufacturer's process, methods, and procedures for design control.; The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) released its Quality System Regulation in 1996, incorporating preproduction design controls and other quality requirements to conform with international quality standards. Device design control requirements were formed by addressing the establishment of performance requirements before device production, compatibility of device components and adequacy of packaging materials. Changes include increased emphasis on involvement of management at all levels of the quality system, record keeping at all stages of production, and clarified controls for handling, sorting, distributing and installing medical devices. The new standards offer manufacturers flexibility in deciding how to comply with the new requirements.},
